Fixed.

**What was wrong.** Not your Python installation as such — the `pip` *package source* was missing. Your default `python3` is `/usr/local/bin/python3` (Python 3.13.1, on Debian 12), and `/usr/local/bin/pip`, `pip3` and `pip3.13` were perfectly good launcher scripts. But `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/` held only the `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` metadata directory with no `.py` files behind it. So every entry point — bare `pip` and `python3 -m pip` alike — died with `No module named pip`. That is why it looked like a broken installation rather than a missing package: the command existed, the thing it imported did not.

A second trap was sitting underneath: `/usr/lib/python3.11/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` is present, so the OS Python 3.11 is PEP 668-locked. Had you fallen back to `/usr/bin/python3 -m pip install`, you would have hit a *different* refusal — `externally-managed-environment` — and concluded the same thing twice for unrelated reasons.

**The repair.** `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` on its own did nothing: it saw the orphaned `dist-info` and reported "Requirement already satisfied" without restoring any files. Removing that stale `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` directory and re-running `ensurepip --upgrade` installed the bundled wheel cleanly (`Successfully installed pip-24.3.1`). No `get-pip.py`, no hand-edits under `/usr/local`, no system Python 3.11 touched.

**Verified, three ways:** `pip 24.3.1 from /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip (python 3.13)`; a throwaway venv installed and imported `six`; and the bare `pip install --dry-run packaging` resolved through to `Would install packaging-26.3`, so index access and resolution both work. No `pip.conf` anywhere, so no stray index-url or proxy.

Two things worth knowing:

- Prefer `python3 -m venv .venv` for real projects. `/usr/local` is a shared interpreter, and a half-deleted package there is exactly the failure you just spent a while on. The venv test passing means you have a clean route regardless.
- One check I could not complete as intended: I sent an independent verifier role to re-test, and it was blocked from the start — that role's `bash_deny` in `.gnomon/roles.toml` refuses `python3?` outright, so it could not run a single Python command. Its failure says nothing about your machine; the repair was confirmed directly instead. If you rely on that verifier for Python projects, that deny pattern will make it useless for anything but a test runner named `pytest`.

